(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-2-exam-1-ent-exam-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Advanced ENT History & Examination Quiz 2 — PD2 Exam 1","category":"Physical Diagnosis","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A patient's own voice is UNUSUALLY LOUD. Which kind of hearing loss does that suggest?","choices":["Neither — loud voice indicates a psychiatric cause","Sensorineural","Conductive — the middle ear amplifies the patient's own voice","Either — voice volume does not distinguish them"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In sensorineural loss the voice may be loud because hearing is difficult, including hearing one's own voice. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What does GENERALIZED lymphadenopathy raise?","choices":["Otitis media and otitis externa","Thyroiditis and goiter","Human immunodeficiency virus","Dental infection and tonsillitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Generalized lymphadenopathy raises human immunodeficiency virus infection, Epstein-Barr virus, lymphoma, leukemia and sarcoidosis. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"Why should dentures be removed during the oral examination?","choices":["To assess the fit of the denture itself","To allow the tongue blade to be inserted","To let the patient speak more clearly during the history","To look underneath for ulcers and lesions"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A denture hides exactly the mucosa most at risk from chronic irritation. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which causes produce CENTRAL vertigo?","choices":["Otitis media, otitis externa and Eustachian tube dysfunction","Brainstem lesion, atherosclerosis and multiple sclerosis","Benign positional vertigo, Ménière disease and labyrinthitis","Loop diuretics, aminoglycosides, salicylates and alcohol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — all vascular, demyelinating or structural problems above the inner ear, with vertebrobasilar migraine and transient ischemic attack completing the list. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A patient says others mumble, and that they struggle most in a noisy restaurant. Which kind of hearing loss does that pattern suggest?","choices":["Sensorineural","Conductive — noise masks the residual hearing","Mixed loss, which always presents this way","Neither — this is a description of tinnitus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sensorineural loss brings trouble understanding speech, complaints that others mumble, and hearing that worsens in noisy environments. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which features mark vertigo as CENTRAL rather than peripheral?","choices":["Hearing impaired on one side with tinnitus present","Episodes lasting under a minute triggered by head position","Aural fullness with fluctuating tinnitus","Brainstem deficits such as dysarthria and ataxia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Central vertigo usually comes with other brainstem deficits (dysarthria, ataxia, crossed motor and sensory deficits), while hearing is unaffected and tinnitus absent. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Why does a septal hematoma require URGENT drainage?","choices":["To prevent secondary sinusitis","To prevent necrosis of the septal cartilage","To prevent the blood tracking into the orbit","To prevent airway obstruction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The cartilage has no blood supply of its own — it depends on the lining that the hematoma has stripped away, so the clock starts immediately. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which cranial nerves carry referred pain to the ear?","choices":["III, IV and VI","VIII alone","XI and XII","V, VII, IX and X"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Four nerves supply sensation to the ear, which is exactly why disease well away from the ear can present as otalgia. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What does the canal look like in ACUTE otitis externa?","choices":["Swollen, narrow, moist, pale and tender, and it may be erythematous","Thickened, red and itchy","Dry, scaly and pale, with no tenderness","Normal, with the abnormality confined to the tympanic membrane"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The swelling and tenderness dominate, and the narrowing is often what makes the examination difficult. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"How do you confirm the otoscope has no leaks BEFORE inserting it for insufflation?","choices":["Compare the bulb pressure against the other otoscope","Place a finger over the speculum tip and squeeze the bulb","Squeeze the bulb in open air and listen for a hiss","Insert the speculum and watch the membrane; no movement means a leak"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. If the bulb squeezes freely with the tip blocked, air is escaping somewhere and the test will be uninterpretable. Resistance means no leak. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"How does a septal hematoma form?","choices":["Chronic inflammation erodes the septum until it bleeds","Venous engorgement of the turbinates spreads to the septum","Injured vessels bleed between the lining and the cartilage","Blood collects within the cartilage itself"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Injury disrupts the blood vessels and pulls the lining away from the cartilage, allowing blood to collect between the two. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which three structures are inspected and palpated before the otoscope is picked up?","choices":["The parotid gland, the temporomandibular joint and the zygomatic arch","The preauricular, postauricular and occipital nodes","The auricles, the mastoid and the tragus","The tympanic membrane, the ossicles and the cochlea"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tenderness found here localizes disease before anything is put into the canal, and tragal tenderness in particular points to the canal itself. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What result on the whispered voice test counts as normal?","choices":["Normal is six of six correct; anything less is abnormal","Normal is one of six correct","Normal is any response at all; the test is qualitative","Normal is three or more of six correct"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Normal is three or more of six correct, and four of six incorrect is abnormal; the three-item sequence is whispered twice. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which pairing of usual cause and accompanying finding fits a tympanic membrane perforation?","choices":["Cerumen impaction, with pain through the perforation","Otitis media, and drainage may come through the perforation","Otitis externa, with itching through the perforation","Barotrauma, with vertigo through the perforation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The perforation is usually the consequence of middle ear disease rather than a primary event. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"How long does benign positional vertigo last overall, as opposed to how long a single episode lasts?","choices":["Episodes last hours, and the condition lasts a few weeks","Episodes and the condition both last a few weeks","Each episode is under a minute, but the condition lasts a few weeks and may recur","Each episode is under a minute, and the condition resolves within 24 hours"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Each episode comes on over a few seconds to under a minute, but the condition itself lasts a few weeks and may recur. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A raised, shiny, slow-growing lesion on the auricle with visible telangiectasia. What should be suspected?","choices":["A tophus","Basal cell carcinoma","A cutaneous cyst","A keloid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Raised, shiny, telangiectatic and slow growing — and malignant, which is what separates it from the benign lumps in the same list. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"The Weber test is done and the patient hears the tone only in the LEFT ear. Which ear has sensorineural hearing loss?","choices":["Neither — this is a normal result","Both","The right","The left"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sensorineural loss lateralizes AWAY from the damaged side, so hearing the tone on the left places the lesion on the right. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A patient speaks unusually SOFTLY despite a hearing complaint. Which loss does that point to?","choices":["Presbycusis specifically","Conductive — the inner ear and cochlear nerve are intact","Sensorineural — the patient compensates by speaking quietly","Mixed loss only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In conductive loss the voice remains soft because the inner ear and cochlear nerve are intact, so the patient's own voice is heard normally. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which statement correctly sorts migraine, tension and cluster headaches by their EPISODIC and UNILATERAL patterns?","choices":["Migraine and cluster are episodic; migraine and tension are unilateral","All three are episodic and all three unilateral","Only cluster is episodic and only tension unilateral","Migraine and tension are episodic; migraine and cluster are unilateral"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Migraine appears in both lists, which is why the two features have to be taken together rather than either alone. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"What kind of pressure is applied to the bulb during pneumatic otoscopy?","choices":["As hard as the patient will tolerate","A series of rapid repeated squeezes","Quick, firm but gentle","Slow and sustained, held for several seconds"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Only a small pressure change is needed; the membrane's movement is slight, and force adds nothing but discomfort. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which description fits a typical tongue cancer?","choices":["On the lateral border or undersurface, as an indurated lesion","On the dorsum, as smooth red patches, in females under thirty","At the tip, as painful ulcers, in any age group","On the undersurface, as blue tortuous swellings"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tongue cancers are usually indurated red or white lesions on the lateral border or undersurface, typically in males over 50. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which causes of hoarseness should a history cover?","choices":["Viral laryngitis, overuse, reflux, nerve damage, smoking","Allergic rhinitis, sinusitis and chronic postnasal drip","Gingivitis and dental caries","Presbycusis and tinnitus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a list that runs from the trivial to the serious, which is why hoarseness always earns a full history. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Why does the ABSENCE of cough count toward the Centor score rather than its presence?","choices":["A cough indicates the infection has spread to the lower airway","Cough is a criterion only in children","A cough points toward a viral cause","Coughing masks tonsillar exudates on examination"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A cough points toward a viral cause, so its absence raises the probability of streptococcal pharyngitis and scores a point. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Necrotizing ulcerative gingivitis is known by which other names?","choices":["Ludwig's angina, and quinsy","Angular cheilitis, and perleche","Aphthous stomatitis, and canker sores","Vincent's angina, and trench mouth"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Necrotizing ulcerative gingivitis is also called Vincent's angina or trench mouth, a painful, severe gum infection with rapid tissue death. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What causes a PERFORATED nasal septum?","choices":["Trauma, surgery or drug use","Allergic rhinitis and chronic sinusitis","A foreign body lodged for several days","Repeated epistaxis from Kiesselbach's plexus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. All three destroy the septal blood supply or the cartilage itself, and drug use is the one patients do not volunteer. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A patient says they are “dizzy”. Which four categories must the history separate?","choices":["Vertigo, migraine, seizure and syncope","Peripheral vertigo, central vertigo, tinnitus and hearing loss","Orthostatic, cardiac, neurological and metabolic","Vertigo, presyncope, disequilibrium and psychiatric causes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The word means nothing until the patient explains it, and these are the four things it turns out to mean. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A tympanic membrane perforation that does NOT extend to the margin is called what?","choices":["Central","Marginal","Attic","Traumatic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The classification is simply by whether the margin is involved, and a marginal perforation is the one that does involve it. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A diffusely enlarged, FIRM thyroid points to what?","choices":["Hashimoto thyroiditis","Graves disease","A multinodular goiter","Thyroid carcinoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Firmness is the feature that separates it from Graves, where the gland is soft. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"What frequency pattern does presbycusis take?","choices":["Higher frequency loss","Lower frequency loss","All frequencies equally","Loss confined to the speech range, sparing higher and lower tones"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it is why the complaint is about clarity rather than volume — the high registers carry the consonants. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What is the Rinne result in CONDUCTIVE hearing loss?","choices":["Bone conduction is heard but air conduction is heard longer","Bone conduction equal to or greater than air conduction","Air conduction greater than bone conduction","Neither is heard at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bone conduction equals or outlasts air conduction, because vibrations through bone bypass the impaired external or middle ear to reach the cochlea. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 44"}]};
